(window.SITE_LANG_DATA = window.SITE_LANG_DATA || {})["zh"] = {"ui":{"skip":"跳到正文","back":"← 返回 AI-gile","lang.button":"语言：{name}","lang.dialog":"选择语言","lang.search":"搜索语言","lang.none":"没有匹配的语言","lang.notYet":"{name} 还没有翻译","theme.aria":"切换主题：浅色、深色或纯黑","theme.toLight":"切换到浅色主题","theme.toDark":"切换到深色主题","theme.toBlack":"切换到纯黑主题（真正的黑色，对 OLED 屏幕友好）","words.underline":"在页面上给这些词加下划线","words.underlineHelp":"把鼠标移到虚线标出的词上或点一下，就能看到它的意思。","words.col.todo":"暂时选定的卡片：团队接下来会从这些开始。","words.col.doing":"有一个 AI 正在处理这张卡片。","words.col.review":"一个 AI 已完成工作，另一个 AI 检查通过后才会交到你手上。","words.col.verify":"等你来：试一试，告诉我们好不好用。","words.col.team":"你的笔记或回答，等团队来接手。","words.col.done":"已完成并通过验收。","index.title":"AI-gile：你的 AI 开发团队，都在一块看板上","index.description":"AI-gile 是给 AI 编程团队用的看板。你睡觉时他们在干活；你只会收到一小堆需要决定、测试和验收的事。","hero.logoAlt":"AI-gile 看板图标","hero.h1.html":"你的 AI 开发团队，都在一块&nbsp;看板上。","hero.alpha":"🐣 封闭预 Alpha","hero.alphaTitle":"还早，也没做完：感谢你来试用！","hero.lede":"你睡觉时他们在开发。醒来只有一小堆事：几个问题，几样要测试的东西，一两颗紫色钻石。点一点，验收，接着去喝你的咖啡。","dl.windows":"Windows 应用","dl.browser":"在浏览器中运行","dl.messenger":"📱 手机版 Messenger","dl.soon":"即将推出","dl.notify":"🔔 开放时通知我","dl.notifySmall":"Alpha 或 Beta","hero.secondary":"敏捷开发，但团队从不需要站会咖啡。（不过他们还是照开站会。）","pitch.h2":"想用几个字介绍 AI-gile，都行","pitch.lede":"选一个长度，从一个字到 144 个字。每个长度都是专门为这个篇幅写的一段介绍。拖动滑块，或用方向键。","pitch.rangeLabel":"字数","pitch.dice":"🎲 换一个版本","pitch.words.one":"{n} 个词","pitch.words.other":"{n} 个词","pitch.letters.one":"{n} 个字母","pitch.letters.other":"{n} 个字母","pitch.chars.one":"{n} 个字","pitch.chars.other":"{n} 个字","count.line.html.one":"看板上现在有 {n} 张卡片","count.line.html.other":"看板上现在有 {n} 张卡片","people.humans.html.one":"👥 {n} 位人类","people.humans.html.other":"👥 {n} 位人类","people.agents.html.one":"🤖 {n} 个智能体","people.agents.html.other":"🤖 {n} 个智能体","people.working.html.one":"⚡ 此刻 {n} 个正在工作","people.working.html.other":"⚡ 此刻 {n} 个正在工作","pre.title":"AI-gile：有东西正在建造","pre.description":"这里正在悄悄建造一些东西：一块让 AI 团队按你的要求来做、还边做边教你的看板。目前是和朋友们一起的封闭预 Alpha。","pre.h1":"这里正在悄悄建造一些东西。 🤫","pre.line":"一块让 AI 团队按你的要求来做、还边做边教你的看板。目前是和朋友们一起的封闭预 Alpha。","pre.btn":"🔔 开放时通知我","pre.peek":"玻璃后面：真实的看板，实时。每张卡片都是一条彩色横条。","pre.peekFake":"玻璃后面：一块正在运转的看板就是这个样子。","pre.night":"🌙 夜间守夜人在掌管一切 · 😴 人类正在休息","features.aria":"它能做什么","f.pile.h":"给你的一小堆","f.pile.html":"<b>待你处理</b>汇集了所有项目里等你的事：问题、要测试的东西、只有人能做的任务。你的队列会告诉你下一件是什么、要花多久。","f.sleep.h":"你睡觉时也在干活的团队","f.sleep.p":"每个项目都有一位负责人和一队 AI 智能体。在卡片上留言，团队就会醒来干活，再带着疲惫的人也能照着做的步骤交还给你。","f.tester.h":"先由 AI 测试员检查","f.tester.p":"东西送到你面前之前，测试员会在应用的副本里照着步骤操作，并附上截图说明看到了什么。通过的快速翻一翻，一键验收。","f.proof.h":"有证据，不画饼","f.proof.p":"每次交付都会列出改了什么：文件、差异、截图。不用逐个打开应用去确认做完没有。","f.chat.h":"和你的负责人聊天","f.chat.p":"类似 Discord 的侧栏：每个项目一个图标，需要你的事用紫色钻石标出。把想法发给负责人，他们会整理成卡片和计划，并附上“✅ 开始”按钮。","f.tidy.h":"自己保持整洁","f.tidy.p":"相关卡片合并成一张，含糊的标题会被改清楚，用量检查让代码保持精简。要切换的东西更少，要操心的也更少。","f.private.h":"归你所有，就在你的电脑上","f.private.li1":"看板运行在你自己的电脑上，只听你的。","f.private.li2":"人与人之间的私信采用端到端加密。","f.private.li3":"没有账号，没有跟踪器，没有 Cookie。","f.lang.h":"说你的语言","f.lang.p":"支持 120 多种语言，从荷兰语、威尔士语、日语，到海盗腔、尤达腔和克林贡语，专业术语还能一键切回英文。","f.service.h":"从一个想法到一项真正的服务","f.service.p":"用 AI 做出了已经有人在用的东西？把它标记为服务，看板就会帮你把它运行好：可用性检查、公开的状态页、附带复盘的事故、需要 Go 才能上线的变更、每天检查的备份。帮助会在你操作时一步步教你。","msgr.h2":"AI-gile Messenger：把团队装进口袋","msgr.p1":"不用打开看板，也能和你的 AI 团队聊天。跟项目负责人和朋友们聊聊，查看卡片的测试步骤并直接回复，全在手机上搞定。","msgr.p2":"扫描看板上的二维码即可配对。第一次用？亲切的演示聊天会带你四处看看。","msgr.when":"先推出 Android 版，接着是适用手机和 iPhone 的网页版。","msgr.who":"你的项目负责人","msgr.b1":"深色模式修好了。要看测试步骤吗？","msgr.b2":"好呀，麻烦了！","msgr.b3":"1. 打开设置。2. 选择深色。看起来对吗？","tl.aria":"从第一次对话到路线图的时间线","tl.h2":"从第一次聊天到接下来的计划","tl.lede":"真实的日期和时间，按你自己的时区显示。今天之后就是计划，不承诺具体日期。","tl.today":"今天","tl.planned":"计划中","tl.doing":"进行中","tl.cards.one":"{n} 张卡片","tl.cards.other":"{n} 张卡片","join.h2":"一起来玩","join.lede":"AI-gile 由一个大胡子怪人（有 AuDHD，跟心理健康打过几场仗）和一大群耐心的智能体打造。目前不公开，只在朋友间封闭内测。开放时想试试吗？","join.btn":"登记兴趣","join.small":"只会给你发邮件，别无其他","footer.tag":"AI-gile。没有跟踪器，没有 Cookie，没有站会咖啡。","footer.licence":"商用许可","footer.status":"状态","footer.privacy":"我们保存什么","footer.thanks":"通话感谢 ChilledChat","form.sending":"发送中…","form.failed":"没成功：请稍后再试。","form.offline":"连不上 AI-gile：请稍后再试。","form.keep":"我们只用它来回复你，之后就会删除。没有跟踪器，没有 Cookie。","form.keepLink":"我们保存什么","interest.title":"AI-gile：Alpha 开放时通知我","interest.description":"留下邮箱，AI-gile 的封闭 Alpha 或 Beta 开放时我们会告诉你。","interest.h1":"🔔 开放时通知我","interest.lede":"AI-gile 正处于封闭预 Alpha 阶段。留下邮箱，等有你的位置时，我们只写一封信。","interest.email":"你的邮箱","interest.which":"哪种测试？","interest.alpha":"封闭 Alpha：早期、粗糙、变化多","interest.beta":"Beta：更稳定，更晚","interest.both":"都行，哪个先开就哪个","interest.submit":"把我加入名单","interest.thanks":"谢谢！你已在名单上。","licence.title":"AI-gile：商用许可","licence.description":"要在公司里使用 AI-gile？可以免费申请商用许可。","licence.h1":"📜 商用许可","licence.lede":"想在工作中使用 AI-gile？告诉我们你是谁、做什么。只要做的事有益处，许可就是免费的。","licence.company":"公司","licence.website":"网站","licence.what":"你做什么","licence.how":"它如何帮助世界","licence.email":"邮箱","licence.submit":"申请","licence.thanks":"谢谢！我们会通过邮件回复。","status.title":"AI-gile 状态","status.h1":"AI-gile 状态","status.quiet":"一切平静","status.intro":"AI-gile 现在正常吗？这个页面每 5 分钟检查一次网站、看板分享、通话和下载。","status.up":"正常运行（{ms} ms 内响应）","status.slow":"上次检查没有响应，正在重试","status.down":"停止运行：最近两次检查都没有响应","status.svc.web":"网站","status.svc.web.offers":"本网站：介绍 AI-gile，以及“开放时通知我”表单","status.svc.boards":"看板分享","status.svc.boards.offers":"公开看板、朋友之间的消息和邀请","status.svc.calls":"通话","status.svc.calls.offers":"AI-gile 里的语音、视频和屏幕共享","status.svc.download":"下载","status.svc.download.offers":"Windows 安装程序和邀请链接","status.checking":"检查中…","status.offline":"暂时连不上状态服务（正在重试）","status.updated":"更新于{ago}","status.justNow":"刚刚","status.min":"{n} 分钟前","status.hours":"{n} 小时前","status.days":"{n} 天前","privacy.title":"AI-gile：我们保存什么","privacy.h1":"我们保存什么","privacy.lede":"简单说：只保存你发给我们的内容，而且不会太久。","privacy.only":"只有你发给我们的内容：你在表单里输入的东西，比如你的邮箱。除此之外，没有任何关于你的信息。","privacy.sealed":"内容会先密封，我们的服务器无法读取：在离开你的浏览器之前就已密封（如果是较旧的浏览器，则在送达的那一刻密封）。只有我们自己的看板能打开它。","privacy.server":"一送达我们的看板，就会从服务器上删除。30 天后仍留在那里的任何内容，也一律删除。","privacy.board":"我们只在回复你之前把它留在看板上，回复后也会从那里删除。","privacy.none":"没有跟踪器，没有 Cookie，没有分析工具。","privacy.local":"你的语言以及浅色或深色的选择只会留在你自己的浏览器里，绝不会发给我们。","privacy.ask":"想早点删除？回复我们的邮件告诉我们就行。","live.h2":"实时看它是怎么建起来的","live.caption":"每张卡片都是隐藏的：你看到的是工作的样子，而不是文字。","live.watching.one":"实时：{n} 人正在看","live.watching.other":"实时：{n} 人正在看","live.last.now":"最近一张卡片刚刚移动","live.last.min.one":"最近一张卡片在 {n} 分钟前移动","live.last.min.other":"最近一张卡片在 {n} 分钟前移动","live.last.hour.one":"最近一张卡片在 {n} 小时前移动","live.last.hour.other":"最近一张卡片在 {n} 小时前移动","live.last.day.one":"最近一张卡片在 {n} 天前移动","live.last.day.other":"最近一张卡片在 {n} 天前移动","live.offline":"实时看板正在稍作休息，请稍后再来看看。","live.cols":"Backlog|待办|进行中|团队检查中|待你测试|交给团队|已完成"},"lines":{"unit":"chars","sizes":[{"n":1,"texts":["看板。","机器人。","放手。"]},{"n":2,"texts":["机器人看板。","智能体，有序。","想法，成真。"]},{"n":3,"texts":["给机器人的看板。","想法变成功能。","免费智能体看板。"]},{"n":5,"texts":["由智能体运转的看板。","你写卡片，智能体来做。","免费、本地、有测试，还有点傻气。"]},{"n":8,"texts":["一块免费的看板，AI 智能体在上面干活。","写下想法，智能体去做，最后由你验收。","给一个人类和一群耐心机器人用的敏捷。"]},{"n":13,"texts":["一块在你电脑上的看板，AI 智能体会自己来领卡片干活。","写下一个想法，一队智能体动手做，再由测试员检查一遍。","免费，没有账号，没有跟踪器：一块看板，几个机器人，你说了算。"]},{"n":21,"texts":["AI-gile 是一块用来和 AI 编程智能体合作的看板。卡片由它们去领，测试和验收则交给你来做。","每个项目都有一位负责人和一队 AI 智能体。你在任意一张卡片上留言，团队就会开始动手干活。","这是一块免费的个人待办看板，就运行在你自己的电脑上。干活的是机器人，点头说好的人是你。"]},{"n":34,"texts":["你把想法放到看板上，一队 AI 智能体把它们做成能用的功能，自己测试一遍，再附上试用步骤交还给你。你没点头之前，没有任何东西算完成。","AI-gile 是一块自制的看板，Rich 在如今被它管理的智能体们的大力帮助下造出了它。它免费，运行在你的电脑上，只分享给朋友，从不出售。","想象一块普通的看板，列从待办排到已完成，只不过卡片是由 AI 智能体来领的。它们会汇报进度，卡住时会提问，而且永远等着你点头同意。"]},{"n":55,"texts":["AI-gile 是一块团队由 AI 智能体组成的看板。每个项目都有一位负责规划的负责人，和几位动手开发的助手。功能做好后，AI 测试员会先试用并截图，然后才放进你的那一小堆。你可以验收，也可以留言，他们就会返工重做。","它起初只是一个人用来同时盯着许多 AI 编程会话的办法，后来慢慢长成了一块像样的看板。它运行在你的电脑上，不需要账号，也没有跟踪器。朋友和好奇的人都可以免费用。它还没做完，所以难免有些毛糙，偶尔也会有小意外。","说句实话：想法还得你来想，结果还得你来看。AI-gile 管的是中间所有的事：让卡片井然有序，你一留言就叫醒该醒的智能体，清楚展示究竟改了什么，再把这一切收进给你的一小堆里。另外它在你的电脑上运行，不要账号，也没有任何跟踪器，完全免费，你想怎么用都行，随时可以停。"]},{"n":89,"texts":["AI-gile 是一块免费的看板，运行在你自己的电脑上。每个项目里干活的不是人，而是一队 AI 智能体：负责人把你的想法整理成卡片和计划，助手则按优先级领取卡片。做好之后，AI 测试员会照着测试步骤操作，并给你看它看到了什么。接着它会放进标着待你处理的那一小堆里等你。你可以验收，也可以留言让团队再来一轮。没有你点头，任何东西都到不了已完成。","有几个你也许想不到的功能。夜班守望会在你睡觉时给待办列补货，专挑不需要你回答的卡片。用量计数器会显示智能体今天和本周用了多少，省用模式则让它们停止消耗。你可以给朋友发消息，私信采用端到端加密。看板支持大约 120 种语言，包括荷兰语、弗拉芒语、海盗腔和克林贡语。这一切一分钱都不收，也没有人想把它卖给你。它运行在你自己的电脑上，不需要账号，也没有跟踪器和 Cookie，数据始终留在你手里。","AI-gile 是一个小小的个人项目，不是公司。Rich 想同时照看好几个 AI 编程项目，又不想挨个打开应用去查看，于是他和智能体们一起造了这块看板，又用这块看板造出了它自己的其余部分。它处于封闭预 Alpha 阶段，意思是对少数几位朋友来说，大体上能用。这里没有价格页，没有新闻邮件，也没有增长计划。如果你觉得有用，等它再开放一些，可以申请试用。"]},{"n":144,"texts":["AI-gile 是给一个人类和许多 AI 智能体用的看板。它运行在你自己的电脑上，完全免费。你添加项目，每个项目都会有一位负责人和一支小团队。你可以和负责人聊天，有点像 Discord，把想法讲给他听；他会把想法整理成卡片和计划，然后等你点下开始。团队按优先级逐张处理卡片，用的是一套专门为语言模型设计的简单命令行。任何东西回到你手里之前，AI 测试员都会在应用的副本里照着步骤操作，并附上截图。做完的工作连同改动的文件和差异，一起落进你的那一小堆。你可以验收，也可以留下一条评论，团队就会醒来再试一次。只有你能把卡片挪到已完成。","大致说说和 AI-gile 度过的一天。傍晚你留下几条评论：这个按钮位置不对，那个列表该排个序，能不能加个深色模式。然后你去睡觉。智能体们读完评论，动手修改，测试，再交还给你。如果待办列空了，夜班守望就从积压清单里补货，挑那些不需要你回答的卡片。早上你面前只有一小堆：几个问题，几样要试的东西，每样都有睡眼惺忪的人也能照做的步骤，还有一张证明它能用的截图。你一路点过去，好的验收，其余的退回，写一句话告诉它们哪里不对就行。用量计数器会告诉你这一切花了多少，节俭的几周还有省用模式可以开。每个项目各有各的负责人，各有各的团队，互不打扰。然后你去吃早餐，这一步智能体暂时还帮不上忙。","先说说 AI-gile 不是什么。它不是创业公司，不对外出售，也没有人在收集你的数据。它是一块免费的看板，运行在你的电脑上，没有账号，没有跟踪器，也没有 Cookie。它是什么呢：一个整洁的地方，能一眼看到所有 AI 编程项目，一队智能体负责做卡片，决定则由你来下。它会把相关的卡片合并，把含糊的标题改清楚，还留意代码是不是保持精简。它支持大约 120 种语言，从荷兰语、弗拉芒语到海盗腔和克林贡语。朋友之间可以私下发消息，而且是加密的。它由 Rich 和一大群耐心的智能体打造，多半是靠它自己来完成的。它仍处于封闭预 Alpha 阶段，有些地方还很粗糙。如果听起来有趣，客气地问一问，也许你能提前进来。"]}],"quips":{"1":"就一个词。够大胆。也许太大胆了。","3":"三个词。已经比大多数产品页更诚实了。","8":"八个词。还是比一次站会短。","21":"顺便说一句，这些长度是故事点数。那当然。","55":"五十五个词。你读这段的工夫，有两张卡片做完了。","144":"这是最长的一版。没有 233 了。智能体已经去睡了。"}},"timeline":{"first-idea.name":"最初的想法","first-idea.line":"第一次对话，那时还没有任何代码。","first-board.name":"第一块看板","first-board.line":"一块记录自己如何被造出来的看板：卡片、列、拖放、项目。","team-roles.name":"团队与角色","team-roles.line":"智能体档案、团队模板、空间，以及谁负责什么。","install.name":"安装与发布","install.line":"朋友也能安装的压缩包、自动更新、渠道和回滚。","usage-saver.name":"用量节省","usage-saver.line":"衡量每次运行的花费，保持代码精简，省下你的额度。","friends.name":"好友与分享","friends.line":"私信、加扰中继、邀请、角色和共享项目。","languages.name":"语言","languages.line":"所有界面文字都放进语言文件，先有荷兰语和弗拉芒语。","ai-tester.name":"AI 测试员","ai-tester.line":"每次交付先由测试员检查，并展示它看到的内容。","website.name":"网站","website.line":"aigile.lol、预告页和这条时间线。","streaming.name":"聊天与直播","streaming.line":"Discord、Twitch、OBS、主播模式、GIF 和表情。","no-ai.name":"无 AI 版","no-ai.line":"给不用 AI 写代码的人用的看板，以及公开看板。","night-watch.name":"夜班守望","night-watch.line":"守夜人和 Scrum Master 在你睡觉时盯着团队干活。","p-more.name":"其他一切","p-more.line":"还在排队等轮到的小想法。","p-android.name":"手机与安卓","p-android.line":"先用手机访问你的看板，再做原生安卓应用。","p-release.name":"Alpha 及以后","p-release.line":"封闭 Alpha、正式上线的网站，然后是 Beta。","p-languages.name":"更多语言","p-languages.line":"130 种语言，还有面向学习者的语言沉浸模式。","p-engines.name":"更多 AI 引擎","p-engines.line":"每个智能体都可以选用 Claude、Codex、Gemini、Copilot 或 Cursor。","p-pro.name":"专业实践","p-pro.line":"风险标签、合并关卡、事故卡片和护栏。","p-self.name":"照顾你自己","p-self.line":"疲惫模式、自我关怀提醒和 4:20。","p-servers.name":"服务器与规模","p-servers.line":"全局服务器设置、角色，以及为增长做的规划。","p-livestream.name":"直播附加功能","p-livestream.line":"Twitch 聊天、自定义表情和公开的直播视图。"},"words":{"button":"📖 专业术语","h":"📖 词语解释","intro":"你在 AI-gile 里会遇到的词，用大白话讲清楚：怎么做出来、怎么安全地改、怎么让它一直为使用者正常运行，以及怎么和 AI 一起作为团队工作。","search":"搜索词语…","none":"没有匹配的词。","close":"关闭","count":{"one":"{count} 个词","other":"{count} 个词"},"groups":[{"id":"idea","name":"💡 Idea and discovery"},{"id":"plan","name":"🗺️ Planning the work"},{"id":"agile","name":"🔁 Agile ways of working"},{"id":"build","name":"🛠️ 做出来"},{"id":"test","name":"🧪 Testing and quality"},{"id":"change","name":"🔀 安全地改动"},{"id":"run","name":"🛎️ 运行一项服务"},{"id":"sec","name":"🔒 Security and privacy"},{"id":"ai","name":"🤖 Working with AI"},{"id":"money","name":"💰 Money and licences"}],"list":[{"id":"discovery","g":"idea","word":"Discovery","text":"The early stage of finding out which problem is worth solving and for whom, before anything gets built.","also":["discovery phase"]},{"id":"problemstmt","g":"idea","word":"Problem statement","text":"One or two sentences saying who has a problem, what it is and why it matters, so everyone aims at the same thing.","also":["problem statements"]},{"id":"user","g":"idea","word":"User","text":"A person who uses what you build. Their needs are what the work is for.","also":["users"],"noTip":1},{"id":"stakeholder","g":"idea","word":"Stakeholder","text":"Anyone who cares how the work turns out or is affected by it, like the people paying for it, using it or supporting it.","also":["stakeholders"]},{"id":"persona","g":"idea","word":"Persona","text":"A made-up but realistic person who stands for one kind of user, so the team can ask “would Sam find this easy?”","also":["personas"]},{"id":"userresearch","g":"idea","word":"User research","text":"Talking to and watching real people to learn what they need, rather than guessing.","also":["user interviews"]},{"id":"journey","g":"idea","word":"User journey","text":"The steps a person takes to get something done, from first hearing about it to finishing, also outside your product. Drawing it out shows where they get stuck.","also":["user journeys","customer journey"]},{"id":"painpoint","g":"idea","word":"Pain point","text":"Something that annoys or slows people down today. Good products take pain points away.","also":["pain points"]},{"id":"assumption","g":"idea","word":"Assumption","text":"Something you believe but haven’t checked yet. Writing assumptions down shows what to test first.","also":["assumptions"],"noTip":1},{"id":"hypothesis","g":"idea","word":"Hypothesis","text":"A best guess you plan to check, with what you expect to happen, like “people will sign up if the first month is free”.","also":["hypotheses"]},{"id":"experiment","g":"idea","word":"Experiment","text":"A small planned test to learn whether an idea works, before betting a lot on it.","also":["experiments"],"noTip":1},{"id":"poc","g":"idea","word":"Proof of concept (PoC)","text":"A quick rough try to see if an idea can work at all. It is usually thrown away once you have the answer.","also":["PoC","proof of concept"]},{"id":"prototype","g":"idea","word":"Prototype","text":"An early model of the product, often clickable pictures, to try the idea on people before building it properly.","also":["prototypes"]},{"id":"wireframe","g":"idea","word":"Wireframe","text":"A simple sketch of a screen showing where things go, without colours or real content.","also":["wireframes"]},{"id":"mockup","g":"idea","word":"Mockup","text":"A detailed picture of how a screen will look, with real colours and text, but nothing works yet.","also":["mockups","mock-up","mock-ups"]},{"id":"bizcase","g":"idea","word":"Business case","text":"The reasons for doing a project: what it costs, what it brings in or saves, and the risks.","also":["business cases"]},{"id":"feasibility","g":"idea","word":"Feasibility","text":"Whether something can really be built and looked after with the time, money and skills you have.","also":["feasible"]},{"id":"outcome","g":"idea","word":"Outcome","text":"The change you want for people, like fewer missed appointments. It matters more than how much you build.","also":["outcomes"],"noTip":1},{"id":"output","g":"idea","word":"Output","text":"Something the team makes, like a new screen or a report. Making it doesn’t automatically mean it helps anyone.","also":["outputs"],"noTip":1},{"id":"successmetric","g":"idea","word":"Success metric","text":"A number that tells you whether the work did what it was for, like more people finishing their order.","also":["success metrics"]},{"id":"kpi","g":"idea","word":"Key performance indicator (KPI)","text":"An important number you watch to see if things are going well, like sign-ups per week.","also":["KPI","KPIs","key performance indicator"]},{"id":"okr","g":"idea","word":"Objectives and key results (OKRs)","text":"A way of setting goals: the objective says what you want, and a few key results are the numbers that show you got there.","also":["OKR","OKRs"]},{"id":"product","g":"plan","word":"Product","text":"The thing being built and looked after for its users, such as an app, a website or a tool.","also":["products"],"noTip":1},{"id":"vision","g":"plan","word":"Product vision","text":"A short statement of who the product helps and where it is heading in the long run.","also":["vision statement"]},{"id":"po","g":"plan","word":"Product owner (PO)","text":"The person who decides what gets built and in what order, so the team always works on what matters most. In AI-gile that is you.","also":["PO","product owners"]},{"id":"pm","g":"plan","word":"Product manager (PM)","text":"The person who looks after a product’s direction: which problems it solves, for whom and why. PM can also mean project manager, so check which.","also":["product managers"]},{"id":"projmgr","g":"plan","word":"Project manager","text":"The person who keeps a project on time and on budget, tracking who does what and by when.","also":["project managers"]},{"id":"roadmap","g":"plan","word":"Roadmap","text":"A rough plan of what is coming over the next months, in order. It shows direction, not exact dates, and changes as you learn.","also":["roadmaps"]},{"id":"milestone","g":"plan","word":"Milestone","text":"A point in a project worth marking, like “first version live” or “100 users”.","also":["milestones"]},{"id":"mvp","g":"plan","word":"Minimum viable product (MVP)","text":"The smallest version of a product that is still useful to real people, so you can learn from them early.","also":["MVP","MVPs","minimum viable product"]},{"id":"requirement","g":"plan","word":"Requirement","text":"Something the finished product must do or be.","also":["requirements"],"noTip":1},{"id":"scope","g":"plan","word":"Scope","text":"What is included in the work and, just as important, what is not.","also":["in scope","out of scope"]},{"id":"scopecreep","g":"plan","word":"Scope creep","text":"When more and more gets added to the work bit by bit, without anyone agreeing on the extra time or cost, so it never quite finishes.","also":["feature creep"]},{"id":"feature","g":"plan","word":"Feature","text":"One thing the product can do for people, like “reset your password”.","also":["features"],"noTip":1},{"id":"epic","g":"plan","word":"Epic","text":"A big piece of work, too large for one go, so it is split into smaller cards.","also":["epics"]},{"id":"story","g":"plan","word":"User story","text":"A small piece of work written from the user’s side: “As a shopper, I want to save my basket, so I can finish later.” It starts a conversation about what to build.","also":["user stories"]},{"id":"ac","g":"plan","word":"Acceptance criteria (AC)","text":"The checks one card must pass to count as done, agreed before work starts, like “the basket is still there after I log out and back in”.","also":["acceptance criteria"]},{"id":"task","g":"plan","word":"Task","text":"A single job to be done, often one small step of a bigger story.","also":["tasks"],"noTip":1},{"id":"ticket","g":"plan","word":"Ticket","text":"Another word for a card: one piece of work or one request, tracked from start to finish.","also":["tickets"]},{"id":"priority","g":"plan","word":"Priority","text":"How important or urgent something is compared with everything else, so the most important work goes first.","also":["priorities"],"noTip":1},{"id":"prioritise","g":"plan","word":"Prioritisation","text":"Putting work in order of importance. A common way is MoSCoW: Must have, Should have, Could have, Won’t have this time.","also":["prioritising","prioritise","MoSCoW"]},{"id":"estimate","g":"plan","word":"Estimate","text":"A best guess at how big or how long a piece of work is. It is a guess, not a promise.","also":["estimates","estimation"],"noTip":1},{"id":"storypoints","g":"plan","word":"Story points","text":"A way of guessing the size of work by comparing cards with each other (“about twice as big as that one”) rather than in hours. They aren’t for comparing teams.","also":["story point"]},{"id":"tshirt","g":"plan","word":"T-shirt sizing","text":"Guessing the size of work as S, M, L or XL. Quick and good enough for early planning.","also":["t-shirt sizes"]},{"id":"poker","g":"plan","word":"Planning poker","text":"A game for estimating: everyone shows a size card at the same time, then the highest and lowest explain why.","also":["Scrum poker"]},{"id":"dependency","g":"plan","word":"Dependency","text":"When one piece of work can’t start or finish until another one does.","also":["dependencies"]},{"id":"blocker","g":"plan","word":"Blocker","text":"Anything stopping work from moving forward right now, like a missing answer or a broken tool.","also":["blockers"]},{"id":"deadline","g":"plan","word":"Deadline","text":"The date something must be finished by.","also":["deadlines"],"noTip":1},{"id":"deliverable","g":"plan","word":"Deliverable","text":"Something concrete the work produces and hands over, like a working page or a report.","also":["deliverables"]},{"id":"forecast","g":"plan","word":"Forecast","text":"A prediction of what is likely to happen, like when the work will be done, based on what you know so far.","also":["forecasts"],"noTip":1},{"id":"metric","g":"plan","word":"Metric","text":"Anything you measure and track over time, like page visits or how long a fix takes.","also":["metrics"]},{"id":"feedback","g":"plan","word":"Feedback","text":"What people tell you about your work, good and bad. Getting it early and often is the heart of Agile.","noTip":1},{"id":"backlog","g":"agile","word":"Backlog","text":"还没排进计划的想法和工作。在移到“待办”之前，那里的内容都不会有人去做。"},{"id":"sprint","g":"agile","word":"迭代","text":"一小段工作周期（通常一到两周），有一组约定要完成的卡片。","also":["Sprint","sprints"]},{"id":"standup","g":"agile","word":"站会","text":"快速碰个头：每位团队成员做了什么、接下来做什么，以及有什么阻碍。","also":["standup","stand-up","stand-ups","Stand-up"]},{"id":"kanban","g":"agile","word":"看板","text":"一种分列（待办、进行中、已完成）的看板，卡片随着工作推进从左向右移动。"},{"id":"wip","g":"agile","word":"WIP 限制","text":"在制品：一列最多该放的卡片数，让团队先把事做完再开新的。","also":["WIP"]},{"id":"dod","g":"agile","word":"完成的定义","text":"团队关于“完成”是什么意思的简短清单，比如已测试、已说明，然后才把卡片交给你。它的双胞胎“就绪的定义”，说的是卡片什么时候清楚到可以开工。","also":["Definition of Done","Definition of Ready","就绪的定义","DoD","DoR"]},{"id":"retro","g":"agile","word":"回顾会","text":"回头看看团队是怎么工作的，商定一件可以做得更好的事。在 AI-gile 里，回顾会会提议改一改团队的工作方式，你说 Go 或者 还不行。","also":["回顾","retro","retros","retrospective","retrospectives"]},{"id":"swarm","g":"agile","word":"蜂群","text":"全员出动：每个团队放下手头的事，一起帮一个紧急项目，直到解决为止。其他项目这段时间先等着。","also":["集中攻关","swarm","swarming"]},{"id":"scrummaster","g":"agile","word":"Scrum Master","text":"让工作保持顺畅的人：发现卡住的卡片，催一催团队，并提醒你有什么在等着。在 AI-gile 里，由首席 Scrum Master 在所有项目里做这件事。","also":["首席 Scrum Master","Lead Scrum Master"]},{"id":"agile","g":"agile","word":"Agile","text":"A way of working in small steps: build a little, show real results often, learn from what people say and change the plan as you go, instead of planning everything up front.","also":["Agile development","agile ways of working"]},{"id":"manifesto","g":"agile","word":"Agile Manifesto","text":"A short statement written in 2001 by software makers. It puts people, working results, close work with customers and responding to change ahead of heavy plans and paperwork.","also":["Manifesto for Agile Software Development"]},{"id":"waterfall","g":"agile","word":"Waterfall","text":"The older way of working: plan everything, then build it all, then test it all, one stage after another. Changes late on are hard and costly.","also":["waterfall model"]},{"id":"iteration","g":"agile","word":"Iteration","text":"One round of building something, checking it and improving it. Agile work is a chain of these, and a sprint is one kind.","also":["iterations"]},{"id":"increment","g":"agile","word":"Increment","text":"A new working piece added to the product in a sprint or round of work, which fits with what was already there.","also":["increments"]},{"id":"scrum","g":"agile","word":"Scrum","text":"A popular Agile way of working: a small team works in short sprints towards a goal, with set roles and a few regular meetings to plan, check and improve."},{"id":"scrumteam","g":"agile","word":"Scrum team","text":"The small group that does the work in Scrum: a product owner, a Scrum Master and the developers.","also":["Scrum teams"]},{"id":"ceremonies","g":"agile","word":"Scrum events","text":"The regular meetings in Scrum: sprint planning, the daily scrum, the sprint review and the retro. Many people call them ceremonies.","also":["ceremonies","Scrum ceremonies"]},{"id":"sprintplan","g":"agile","word":"Sprint planning","text":"The meeting at the start of a sprint where the team agrees why the sprint matters, which cards to take on and how to start.","also":["planning meeting"]},{"id":"sprintgoal","g":"agile","word":"Sprint goal","text":"One sentence saying what a sprint is for. Small tasks can change along the way, as long as the goal is met.","also":["sprint goals"]},{"id":"sprintbacklog","g":"agile","word":"Sprint backlog","text":"The cards picked for the current sprint, with the team’s plan for doing them.","also":["sprint backlogs"]},{"id":"productbacklog","g":"agile","word":"Product backlog","text":"The full ordered list of everything that might be built for the product, most important at the top. It keeps changing as you learn.","also":["product backlogs"]},{"id":"productgoal","g":"agile","word":"Product goal","text":"The bigger result the product is working towards, which helps the team choose what to do next.","also":["product goals"]},{"id":"refinement","g":"agile","word":"Backlog refinement","text":"Regularly going through the backlog to tidy, split, order and add detail to cards, so they are ready to pick up. Also called backlog grooming.","also":["backlog grooming","refinement"]},{"id":"sprintreview","g":"agile","word":"Sprint review","text":"A meeting at the end of a sprint where the team shows what it finished and hears what people think, to decide what comes next.","also":["sprint reviews"]},{"id":"demo","g":"agile","word":"Demo","text":"Showing finished work working, live, to the people who asked for it.","also":["demos"],"noTip":1},{"id":"timebox","g":"agile","word":"Timebox","text":"A fixed amount of time for something. When it runs out you stop, even if not everything is done.","also":["timeboxed","timeboxes"]},{"id":"velocity","g":"agile","word":"Velocity","text":"How much work a team usually finishes in a sprint, used to plan how much to take on next time. It isn’t a score for how hard people work.","also":["team velocity"]},{"id":"capacity","g":"agile","word":"Capacity","text":"How much work the team can take on in a period, after holidays and other jobs.","noTip":1},{"id":"burndown","g":"agile","word":"Burndown chart","text":"A chart of the work left in a sprint going down day by day. If the line stays flat, something is stuck.","also":["burndown","burn-down chart"]},{"id":"burnup","g":"agile","word":"Burnup chart","text":"A chart of finished work going up over time, next to the total planned, so you can see when you will get there.","also":["burnup","burn-up chart"]},{"id":"kanbanboard","g":"agile","word":"Kanban board","text":"The board itself: a column for each stage of the work, with cards moving across.","also":["Kanban boards"]},{"id":"pullsys","g":"agile","word":"Pull system","text":"A way of working where new work only starts when someone has room to take it on, rather than being pushed onto a full plate.","also":["pull-based"]},{"id":"flow","g":"agile","word":"Flow","text":"How smoothly work moves across the board. Good flow means cards keep moving rather than piling up.","also":["flow of work"],"noTip":1},{"id":"leadtime","g":"agile","word":"Lead time","text":"How long it takes from someone asking for something to it being finished.","also":["lead times"]},{"id":"cycletime","g":"agile","word":"Cycle time","text":"How long a card takes from when work really starts on it to when it is finished.","also":["cycle times"]},{"id":"throughput","g":"agile","word":"Throughput","text":"How many cards get finished in a week, or in any set time.","also":["throughput rate"]},{"id":"bottleneck","g":"agile","word":"Bottleneck","text":"The one stage where work piles up and slows everything behind it, like the narrow neck of a bottle.","also":["bottlenecks"]},{"id":"swimlane","g":"agile","word":"Swimlane","text":"A row across the board that groups cards of one kind, such as urgent work or one team’s cards.","also":["swimlanes","swim lane"]},{"id":"lean","g":"agile","word":"Lean","text":"A way of working that cuts out waste (anything that doesn’t help the customer) and keeps work flowing."},{"id":"waste","g":"agile","word":"Waste","text":"In Lean, anything that takes effort but doesn’t help the customer, like waiting, rework or building things nobody uses.","noTip":1},{"id":"kaizen","g":"agile","word":"Continuous improvement","text":"Getting a little better all the time through small regular changes, then checking they helped. Retros are one way to do it. Also called kaizen.","also":["kaizen"]},{"id":"selforg","g":"agile","word":"Self-organising team","text":"A team that decides for itself how to do the work, rather than being told step by step.","also":["self-organising","self-managing team"]},{"id":"crossfunc","g":"agile","word":"Cross-functional team","text":"A team that has all the skills it needs to finish the work itself, like design, building and testing.","also":["cross-functional"]},{"id":"spike","g":"agile","word":"Spike","text":"A short, timeboxed bit of research to answer a question before committing to the real work.","also":["spikes"]},{"id":"rework","g":"agile","word":"Rework","text":"Doing work again because it wasn’t right the first time.","also":["reworked"]},{"id":"safe","g":"agile","word":"Scaled Agile (SAFe)","text":"Ways of using Agile across many teams in a big organisation. SAFe (the Scaled Agile Framework) is one well known example.","also":["SAFe","scaled Agile"]},{"id":"repo","g":"build","word":"仓库","text":"项目的代码文件夹及其完整的提交历史，通常也保存在 GitHub 上。","also":["repository","Repo","repo"]},{"id":"branch","g":"build","word":"分支","text":"代码中一条独立的工作线，这样就能在不碰主版本的情况下做改动。","also":["branches","Branch"]},{"id":"commit","g":"build","word":"提交","text":"代码的一次已保存快照，附有一句改动说明。你随时可以回到某一个快照。","also":["Commit","commit","commits"]},{"id":"push","g":"build","word":"推送","text":"把已保存的快照（提交）从这台电脑发送到 GitHub，这样它们就有了备份并可共享。"},{"id":"merge","g":"build","word":"合并","text":"把一个分支的改动并回主版本。"},{"id":"pr","g":"build","word":"拉取请求","text":"请求检查某个分支的改动并合并到主版本。","also":["PR","Pull request"]},{"id":"refactor","g":"build","word":"重构","text":"在不改变功能的前提下整理代码：文件更小、命名更清晰、重复更少。"},{"id":"bug","g":"build","word":"Bug","text":"某样东西没有按预期正常工作。","also":["bugs","缺陷"]},{"id":"peerreview","g":"build","word":"同行评审","text":"多一双眼睛：另一个 AI 会在每张卡片的工作到达你之前检查一遍，这样漏掉的错误更少。","also":["代码评审","peer review","code review"]},{"id":"code","g":"build","word":"Code","text":"The written instructions that tell a computer what to do. Also called source code.","also":["source code"],"noTip":1},{"id":"proglang","g":"build","word":"Programming language","text":"A language for writing code, such as JavaScript, Python or Java. Different ones suit different jobs.","also":["programming languages"]},{"id":"developer","g":"build","word":"Developer (dev)","text":"Someone who writes and changes code. Also called a programmer or software engineer.","also":["developer","developers","programmer","programmers","software engineer"]},{"id":"software","g":"build","word":"Software","text":"Programs that run on computers and phones, as opposed to the machines themselves (the hardware).","noTip":1},{"id":"app","g":"build","word":"App","text":"Short for application: a program people use to get something done, on a phone, a computer or in a web browser.","also":["apps","application","applications"],"noTip":1},{"id":"webapp","g":"build","word":"Web app","text":"An app that runs in a web browser, so there is nothing to install.","also":["web apps","web application"]},{"id":"frontend","g":"build","word":"Front end","text":"The part of an app you see and click on: the screens, buttons and words, often in your browser.","also":["front-end","frontend"]},{"id":"backend","g":"build","word":"Back end","text":"The behind-the-scenes part of an app that keeps the data and does the work when you click something, like checking your password.","also":["back-end","backend"]},{"id":"fullstack","g":"build","word":"Full stack","text":"Working on both the front end and the back end.","also":["full-stack"]},{"id":"ui","g":"build","word":"User interface (UI)","text":"What a person sees and uses on screen: layout, buttons, menus and text.","also":["UI","user interface"]},{"id":"ux","g":"build","word":"User experience (UX)","text":"How it feels to use something: easy, clear and pleasant, or confusing and slow.","also":["UX","user experience"]},{"id":"accessibility","g":"build","word":"Accessibility (a11y)","text":"Making things usable by everyone, including people who can’t see well, can’t use a mouse or use a screen reader.","also":["a11y"]},{"id":"responsive","g":"build","word":"Responsive design","text":"Making pages fit and work well on any screen size, from a phone to a big monitor.","also":["responsive"]},{"id":"designsys","g":"build","word":"Design system","text":"A shared kit of colours, fonts, buttons and rules, so every screen looks and behaves the same way.","also":["design systems"]},{"id":"architecture","g":"build","word":"Architecture","text":"The overall shape of a system: what the main parts are and how they talk to each other."},{"id":"database","g":"build","word":"Database (DB)","text":"An organised store of information, like a very powerful spreadsheet that the app can read and change.","also":["database","databases","DB"]},{"id":"schema","g":"build","word":"Database schema","text":"The layout of a database: which tables of information it has and what goes in each.","also":["schema"]},{"id":"sql","g":"build","word":"SQL","text":"Short for structured query language: the language used to ask many databases for information or to change it.","also":["Structured Query Language"]},{"id":"data","g":"build","word":"Data","text":"Information kept by a computer, like names, orders or settings.","also":["dataset","datasets"],"noTip":1},{"id":"server","g":"build","word":"Server","text":"A computer that is always on and answers requests from other computers, like sending a web page when you visit it.","also":["servers"]},{"id":"client","g":"build","word":"Client","text":"The side that asks for something, usually your browser or app, talking to a server. In business it also just means a customer.","also":["clients"],"noTip":1},{"id":"api","g":"build","word":"API","text":"Short for application programming interface: a set menu of requests one program can make to another, like asking a weather service for today’s forecast.","also":["APIs","application programming interface"]},{"id":"endpoint","g":"build","word":"Endpoint","text":"One item on an API’s menu: a web address a program can call to get or send something.","also":["endpoints"]},{"id":"integration","g":"build","word":"Integration","text":"Connecting two systems so they work together, like your shop sending orders to your accounts software.","also":["integrations"]},{"id":"webhook","g":"build","word":"Webhook","text":"A message one system sends to another by itself the moment something happens, like “payment received”.","also":["webhooks"]},{"id":"library","g":"build","word":"Library","text":"Ready-made code someone else wrote that you can reuse instead of writing it yourself.","also":["libraries"],"noTip":1},{"id":"framework","g":"build","word":"Framework","text":"A ready-made structure for building an app that decides much of how the code is laid out, like a house frame you fill in.","also":["frameworks"]},{"id":"sdk","g":"build","word":"Software development kit (SDK)","text":"A box of tools, code and examples for building software for one platform or service.","also":["SDK","SDKs"]},{"id":"softdep","g":"build","word":"Software dependency","text":"A library or tool your code needs to work. Keeping them up to date matters for safety.","also":["software dependencies"]},{"id":"package","g":"build","word":"Package","text":"A bundle of ready-made code you can install and use in a project.","also":["packages"],"noTip":1},{"id":"pkgmgr","g":"build","word":"Package manager","text":"A tool that fetches and updates the packages your code depends on. npm is a common one.","also":["package managers","npm"]},{"id":"git","g":"build","word":"Git","text":"The most widely used tool for keeping the history of code changes, as commits and branches."},{"id":"github","g":"build","word":"GitHub","text":"A website that keeps Git repos online, so teams can share code, review changes and track work."},{"id":"vcs","g":"build","word":"Version control","text":"Keeping a full history of every change to the files, so you can see who changed what and go back if needed. Git does this.","also":["source control","version control system"]},{"id":"clone","g":"build","word":"Clone","text":"Making your own full copy of a repo, with its history, on your computer.","also":["cloned","cloning"]},{"id":"pull","g":"build","word":"Pull","text":"Fetching the latest commits from the shared copy (like GitHub) onto your computer. The opposite of push.","also":["git pull"],"noTip":1},{"id":"mainbranch","g":"build","word":"Main branch","text":"The official version of the code that everyone works from, often called main or master.","also":["master branch"]},{"id":"conflict","g":"build","word":"Merge conflict","text":"When two changes touch the same lines in different ways, so the computer can’t join them and a person has to choose.","also":["merge conflicts"]},{"id":"diff","g":"build","word":"Diff","text":"A view showing exactly what changed between two versions: lines added and lines taken away.","also":["diffs"]},{"id":"fork","g":"build","word":"Fork","text":"Your own copy of someone else’s repo, which you can change without affecting theirs.","also":["forks","forked"]},{"id":"script","g":"build","word":"Script","text":"A short program that does one job by itself, like tidying files or starting the app.","also":["scripts"],"noTip":1},{"id":"build","g":"build","word":"Build","text":"Turning the code into something ready to run, or the ready-to-run result itself.","also":["builds"],"noTip":1},{"id":"compile","g":"build","word":"Compile","text":"Translating code people wrote into a form the computer can run directly.","also":["compiled","compiler","compiling"]},{"id":"cli","g":"build","word":"Command line (CLI)","text":"A text window where you type instructions for the computer instead of clicking. Also called the terminal.","also":["command line","command-line","CLI","terminal"]},{"id":"config","g":"build","word":"Configuration (config)","text":"The settings that control how software behaves, kept apart from the code so they are easy to change.","also":["config","configuration"],"noTip":1},{"id":"envvar","g":"build","word":"Environment variable","text":"A setting handed to a program when it starts, often used for addresses or passwords so they aren’t written into the code.","also":["environment variables","env var"]},{"id":"debugging","g":"build","word":"Debugging","text":"Hunting down why something isn’t working, and fixing it.","also":["debug","debugged"]},{"id":"pairing","g":"build","word":"Pair programming","text":"Two people (or a person and an AI) working on the same code together, one typing and one checking.","also":["pairing"]},{"id":"docs","g":"build","word":"Documentation (docs)","text":"Written explanations of how something works or how to use it.","also":["documentation","docs"],"noTip":1},{"id":"readme","g":"build","word":"README","text":"The first file to read in a repo: what the project is and how to get it running.","also":["READMEs"]},{"id":"legacy","g":"build","word":"Legacy system","text":"An older system still in use that is hard to change, often because few people still understand it.","also":["legacy systems","legacy code"]},{"id":"techdebt","g":"build","word":"Technical debt","text":"Shortcuts that save time now but make later changes slower, like a loan that builds up interest. Refactoring pays it down.","also":["tech debt"]},{"id":"test","g":"test","word":"测试","text":"在你接受之前，试用一项完成的工作，检查它是否符合要求。","also":["tests","Test"],"noTip":1},{"id":"qa","g":"test","word":"QA","text":"quality assurance（质量保证）的缩写：任何东西上线前都会自动运行的检查。QA 变红就说明有检查没通过，修好之前不能上线。","also":["质量检查","质量保证","quality assurance","quality checks"]},{"id":"testpile","g":"test","word":"测试队列","text":"等你来试的已完成卡片，一张接一张。接受一张，或者附上说明退回去，下一张就出来了。","also":["test pile","test piles"]},{"id":"autotest","g":"test","word":"Automated test","text":"A check the computer runs by itself every time the code changes, to catch mistakes early.","also":["automated tests","automatic tests"]},{"id":"manualtest","g":"test","word":"Manual test","text":"A person trying the work by hand to see if it behaves as it should.","also":["manual tests","manual testing"]},{"id":"unittest","g":"test","word":"Unit test","text":"A small automatic check of one tiny piece of code on its own.","also":["unit tests"]},{"id":"integtest","g":"test","word":"Integration test","text":"A check that several parts work properly together, not just one at a time.","also":["integration tests"]},{"id":"endtoend","g":"test","word":"End-to-end test (E2E)","text":"A test that acts like a real user from start to finish, such as signing up, buying and paying.","also":["E2E","end-to-end","end-to-end tests"]},{"id":"uat","g":"test","word":"User acceptance testing (UAT)","text":"The people who asked for the work try it themselves and say whether it does what they need. AI-gile’s test pile is its version of this.","also":["UAT","acceptance testing"]},{"id":"regression","g":"test","word":"Regression","text":"Something that used to work but broke after a change. Regression tests check that the old things still work.","also":["regressions","regression test","regression tests","regression testing"]},{"id":"smoke","g":"test","word":"Smoke test","text":"A very quick check that the main things work at all, for example right after a change goes live. If it fails, stop and look.","also":["smoke tests"]},{"id":"exploratory","g":"test","word":"Exploratory testing","text":"Trying the product freely and poking at it in different ways, to find problems that planned checks would miss.","also":["exploratory test","exploratory tests"]},{"id":"testcase","g":"test","word":"Test case","text":"One written scenario to try: the steps, and what should happen.","also":["test cases"]},{"id":"coverage","g":"test","word":"Test coverage","text":"How much of the code is checked by automatic tests. More is usually safer, but high coverage alone doesn’t prove it works well.","also":["coverage","code coverage"]},{"id":"testdata","g":"test","word":"Test data","text":"Made-up example information used while testing, so no real person’s private details are exposed.","also":["test fixtures"]},{"id":"edgecase","g":"test","word":"Edge case","text":"An unusual situation at the limits, like an empty form or a name with an apostrophe, where things often break.","also":["edge cases"]},{"id":"happypath","g":"test","word":"Happy path","text":"The normal route where everything goes right. Good testing also tries the unhappy paths."},{"id":"perftest","g":"test","word":"Performance testing","text":"Checking how fast something is and how well it copes under strain.","also":["performance test","performance tests"]},{"id":"loadtest","g":"test","word":"Load testing","text":"Checking how a system copes when lots of people use it at once.","also":["load test","load tests"]},{"id":"sectest","g":"test","word":"Security testing","text":"Looking for weaknesses that could let someone misuse a system or see what they shouldn’t.","also":["security test","security tests"]},{"id":"accesstest","g":"test","word":"Accessibility testing","text":"Checking that people with different abilities can use the product, including with a screen reader or only a keyboard.","also":["accessibility test"]},{"id":"linting","g":"test","word":"Linting","text":"An automatic check that spots likely mistakes and messy style in code, like a spelling checker for programs.","also":["linter","lint"]},{"id":"static","g":"test","word":"Static analysis","text":"Checking code for problems by reading it automatically, without running it.","also":["static code analysis"]},{"id":"flaky","g":"test","word":"Flaky test","text":"A test that sometimes passes and sometimes fails for no clear reason, so it can’t be fully trusted.","also":["flaky tests"]},{"id":"repro","g":"test","word":"Reproduce (repro)","text":"Making a bug happen again on purpose by following the same steps, so it can be studied and fixed. Writing those steps down is the most useful part of a bug report.","also":["repro","reproduce","steps to reproduce"]},{"id":"severity","g":"test","word":"Severity","text":"How bad a problem’s effect is, from a small annoyance to everything stopping.","also":["severity level"]},{"id":"deploy","g":"change","word":"部署","text":"让新版本上线，例如放到人们使用的服务器上。"},{"id":"release","g":"change","word":"发布","text":"一批有名字、一起上线的已完成工作，附带几句简短说明，讲清改了什么。🚀 发布会根据已完成的卡片写好这些说明。","also":["发布版本","release","releases"]},{"id":"rollback","g":"change","word":"回滚","text":"新版本出问题时，退回到上一个正常工作的版本。在 AI-gile 里，它会把每项变更上写好的撤销步骤汇成一份清单。","also":["回退","rollback","roll back","roll-back","rolled back"]},{"id":"live","g":"change","word":"线上","text":"大家真正在用的那个版本，而不是用来试东西的副本。把东西放上去就叫上线。开发者也叫它生产环境。","also":["上线","生产环境","live","production"],"noTip":1},{"id":"change","g":"change","word":"变更","text":"对大家依赖的东西做的有计划的改动，事先写清楚：改什么、为什么、有什么风险、什么时候改、怎么撤销。用 🔀 提出变更 来开始。","also":["变更请求","change","change request","change requests"],"noTip":1},{"id":"risk","g":"change","word":"风险","text":"可能出错的事，按发生的可能性和严重程度打分。⚠️ 风险 把它们放在一个列表里，分数最高的在最前面，每条都有一个再次查看的日期。","also":["风险登记","risk","risks","risk register"]},{"id":"chgwin","g":"change","word":"变更窗口","text":"允许变更上线的时间，比如一个清闲的周二晚上。在窗口之外，变更卡片无法完成，除非是紧急情况。","also":["变更时段","change window","change windows"]},{"id":"gonogo","g":"change","word":"Go / no go","text":"变更上线前的一次快速表决：每个需要审批的人说 Go 或 No go。只有大家都同意才继续。","also":["go/no go","go-no-go","上线决策"]},{"id":"approval","g":"change","word":"审批","text":"开工前先等一句“可以”。朋友提的卡片，或者一项变更，会在 ✅ 审批 里等着，直到你批准或拒绝。","also":["批准","approval","approvals"]},{"id":"quiettime","g":"change","word":"静默时段","text":"一段什么都不上线的时间，比如直播、演示或夜里。团队照常工作和测试，但上线要等它结束。别的地方叫它变更冻结。","also":["变更冻结","封版","quiet time","quiet times","change freeze"]},{"id":"devops","g":"change","word":"DevOps","text":"A way of working where the people who build software and the people who run it work as one team, with lots of automation, so changes go live often and safely."},{"id":"cicd","g":"change","word":"CI/CD (continuous integration and delivery)","text":"Everyone’s changes are joined into the main version often and checked automatically each time (continuous integration), so the software is always ready to go live at the press of a button (continuous delivery).","also":["CI/CD","CI","CD","continuous integration","continuous delivery"]},{"id":"contdeploy","g":"change","word":"Continuous deployment","text":"One step further than continuous delivery: every change that passes the checks goes live by itself, with no button to press.","also":["continuous deployments"]},{"id":"pipeline","g":"change","word":"Pipeline","text":"The automatic series of steps a change goes through on its way to live, like build, test, then deploy.","also":["pipelines"]},{"id":"environment","g":"change","word":"Environment","text":"One separate copy of the system for one purpose, such as one to build in, one to test in and the live one.","also":["environments"],"noTip":1},{"id":"devenv","g":"change","word":"Development environment","text":"The copy a developer works in, often on their own computer, where breaking things harms nobody.","also":["dev environment"]},{"id":"testenv","g":"change","word":"Test environment","text":"A separate copy of the system used just for testing, so the real service isn’t touched.","also":["test environments"]},{"id":"staging","g":"change","word":"Staging","text":"A last copy set up just like live, for a final check before a change goes live.","also":["staging environment"]},{"id":"artifact","g":"change","word":"Build artifact","text":"The file or bundle a build produces, ready to be stored, checked or put live.","also":["build artifacts"]},{"id":"hotfix","g":"change","word":"Hotfix","text":"An urgent fix put live quickly, outside the normal plan, because something important is broken.","also":["hotfixes"]},{"id":"patch","g":"change","word":"Patch","text":"A small update that fixes a bug or closes a security hole. Applying security patches quickly matters.","also":["patches","security patch","security patches"]},{"id":"version","g":"change","word":"Version number","text":"A label like 2.4.1 that says which release you have. Often the first number changes for big changes and the last for small fixes.","also":["version numbers","semantic versioning"]},{"id":"relnotes","g":"change","word":"Release notes","text":"A short plain list of what changed in a release, for the people who use it, including fixes and known limits. Also called a changelog.","also":["changelog","change log"]},{"id":"flag","g":"change","word":"Feature flag","text":"An on and off switch for a feature, so new work can go live hidden and be turned on (or off again) without a new release.","also":["feature flags","feature toggle","feature toggles"]},{"id":"rollfwd","g":"change","word":"Roll forward","text":"Fixing a bad release by quickly putting out a new, corrected version instead of going back to the old one.","also":["rolling forward","rolled forward"]},{"id":"canary","g":"change","word":"Canary release","text":"Putting a change live for a small group first, and for everyone only once it looks fine. Named after the canaries miners took underground as an early warning.","also":["canary releases","canary"]},{"id":"bluegreen","g":"change","word":"Blue-green deployment","text":"Keeping two copies of the live system and switching people over to the new one in one go, so you can switch straight back if it goes wrong.","also":["blue-green","blue green deployment"]},{"id":"zerodown","g":"change","word":"Zero-downtime deployment","text":"Putting a new version live without the service ever stopping for its users.","also":["zero downtime","zero-downtime"]},{"id":"chgenable","g":"change","word":"Change enablement","text":"Helping changes succeed: weighing the risk, getting the right approval and picking a good time. Also called change management.","also":["change management"]},{"id":"cab","g":"change","word":"Change advisory board (CAB)","text":"A group that looks over important changes and agrees whether and when they may go ahead.","also":["CAB"]},{"id":"stdchange","g":"change","word":"Standard change","text":"A low-risk change done the same tried and tested way each time, so it is approved in advance.","also":["standard changes"]},{"id":"normchange","g":"change","word":"Normal change","text":"A change that needs to be checked and approved before it goes ahead.","also":["normal changes"]},{"id":"emergchange","g":"change","word":"Emergency change","text":"A change that must happen right away to fix or prevent serious trouble, with a quicker approval to match.","also":["emergency changes"]},{"id":"automation","g":"change","word":"Automation","text":"Getting the computer to do a repeated job by itself, so it is quicker and done the same way every time.","also":["automated"],"noTip":1},{"id":"infra","g":"change","word":"Infrastructure","text":"The machines, networks and services that software runs on.","also":["infra"]},{"id":"iac","g":"change","word":"Infrastructure as code (IaC)","text":"Describing your servers and settings in files kept with the code, so they can be rebuilt exactly the same way every time.","also":["IaC"]},{"id":"cloud","g":"change","word":"Cloud","text":"Renting computers, storage and services from a big provider over the internet, instead of owning the machines yourself.","also":["the cloud","cloud computing"]},{"id":"provider","g":"change","word":"Cloud provider","text":"A company that rents out computing over the internet, such as Amazon Web Services (AWS), Microsoft Azure or Google Cloud.","also":["cloud providers"]},{"id":"hosting","g":"change","word":"Hosting","text":"Where your app or website lives and runs so people can reach it, usually rented from a company.","also":["hosted"]},{"id":"domain","g":"change","word":"Domain name","text":"The web address people type, like example.com. You rent it by the year, so it must be renewed.","also":["domain names","domain"]},{"id":"dns","g":"change","word":"DNS","text":"Short for domain name system: the internet’s address book, which turns a name like example.com into the address of the right computer.","also":["domain name system"]},{"id":"container","g":"change","word":"Container","text":"A sealed package holding an app and everything it needs, so it runs the same on any computer. Docker is the best known tool.","also":["containers","Docker"],"noTip":1},{"id":"vm","g":"change","word":"Virtual machine (VM)","text":"A pretend computer running inside a real one, so one big machine can act as many.","also":["VM","VMs","virtual machines"]},{"id":"serverless","g":"change","word":"Serverless","text":"Running code without looking after any servers yourself: the provider runs it when needed and you pay for what you use."},{"id":"lb","g":"change","word":"Load balancer","text":"Something that spreads visitors across several servers, so no single one gets overwhelmed.","also":["load balancers"]},{"id":"cache","g":"change","word":"Cache","text":"A handy store of recent results kept close by, so the same thing doesn’t have to be fetched or worked out again. Clearing it can fix pages that look out of date.","also":["caches","caching","cached"]},{"id":"cdn","g":"change","word":"Content delivery network (CDN)","text":"A worldwide set of servers holding copies of your pages and pictures near your visitors, so they load quickly.","also":["CDN"]},{"id":"migration","g":"change","word":"Migration","text":"Moving data or systems from an old setup to a new one, or carefully changing how a database is laid out.","also":["migrations","database migration"]},{"id":"service","g":"run","word":"服务","text":"你做出来、大家依赖的东西，比如网站或应用，有人照看着让它一直正常运行。勾选“此项目是一项服务”，再写上它提供什么、谁在用、谁值班。","also":["service","services"]},{"id":"uptimepct","g":"run","word":"Uptime","text":"How much of the time a service is working, usually as a percentage. 99.9% still allows about 43 minutes down a month.","also":["uptime percentage"]},{"id":"uptime","g":"run","word":"可用性检查","text":"每隔几分钟自动检查一次你的服务是否还在响应。如果连续失败几次，AI-gile 会自己开一张事故卡片。","also":["在线检查","可用性","uptime","uptime check","uptime checks"]},{"id":"statuspage","g":"run","word":"状态页","text":"一个公开页面，显示服务是否正常（绿、橙或红）以及最新进展，让使用者自己查看，不用来问。","also":["状态页面","status page","status pages"]},{"id":"desk","g":"run","word":"服务台","text":"使用你服务的人提出的问题和反馈都会汇集到这里。AI-gile 会把每一条归类为请求、事故或想法，如果看起来和已经反馈过的东西很像，也会告诉你。","also":["服务台","帮助台","help desk","helpdesk","service desk"]},{"id":"svcrequest","g":"run","word":"服务请求","text":"有人要的是普通的东西，比如新账号或一点帮助，而不是反馈有东西坏了。它会排队等候；事故优先处理。","also":["服务请求","请求","service request","service requests"]},{"id":"incident","g":"run","word":"事故","text":"此刻正在影响使用者的故障，比如网站打不开。🔥 事故卡片会插队：先让它恢复正常再说。","also":["故障","incident","incidents"]},{"id":"major","g":"run","word":"重大事故","text":"大事故：影响很多人，或者钱或数据有危险。一个按钮就能让所有团队都扑上去（蜂群），叫醒值班的人，并要求每 30 分钟更新一次状态。","also":["重大事件","重大故障","major incident","major incidents"]},{"id":"oncall","g":"run","word":"值班","text":"就是出问题时负责响应的人，哪怕是半夜。每项服务都有一位值班的团队成员，它的事故会最先叫醒这个人。","also":["值班人员","待命","on call","on-call"]},{"id":"replyby","g":"run","word":"回复期限","text":"一张卡片最多能等多久得到第一次回复，比如事故是一小时。超过之后它会出现一个红色时钟，免得有事被悄悄忘掉。也叫响应目标。","also":["响应目标","响应时间","reply-by time","response target","response targets"]},{"id":"postmortem","g":"run","word":"复盘","text":"事故之后的简短回顾：发生了什么、什么起了作用、要改什么，不追究任何人的责任。没有复盘，事故就不能关闭。","also":["事故复盘","事后复盘","post-mortem","post-mortems","postmortem","post-incident review"]},{"id":"problem","g":"run","word":"问题","text":"一个或多个事故背后的隐藏原因。🧩 问题卡片会把反复出现的事故归到一起，好找到原因并彻底修好。","also":["problem","problems"],"noTip":1},{"id":"rootcause","g":"run","word":"根本原因","text":"东西坏掉的真正原因，而不只是你看到的表面故障。修好根本原因，事故就不会再回来。","also":["根因","root cause","root causes"]},{"id":"workaround","g":"run","word":"临时解决办法","text":"在真正的修复做好之前让东西继续运转的办法，比如卡住时重启一下。它会写在问题卡片上，这样谁都不用再摸索一遍。","also":["临时方案","绕过办法","workaround","workarounds"]},{"id":"knownerror","g":"run","word":"已知错误","text":"原因已经搞清楚、也有临时解决办法，但还没修好的问题。🧩 已知错误 把它们列出来，大家直接用临时解决办法，不用再重新排查。","also":["known error","known errors"]},{"id":"runbook","g":"run","word":"运行手册","text":"处理某类已知问题的分步说明，让每次都用同样的方式快速搞定。重大事故之后，团队可以起草一份运行手册，交给你确认。","also":["操作手册","runbook","runbooks"]},{"id":"ci","g":"run","word":"配置项","text":"你的服务所依赖的某样东西，比如服务器、域名、证书、密钥或供应商。🗂️ 配置项按服务把它们列出来，让你看清一起事故会影响到什么，以及哪些东西需要续期或备份。","also":["配置项","配置条目","configuration item","configuration items","CI"]},{"id":"expiry","g":"run","word":"到期日","text":"AI-gile 会留意各种日期，比如证书或域名什么时候到期。在到期前 30 天内，它会创建一张卡片提醒你续期，免得东西突然停止工作。","also":["到期日","到期","到期提醒","expiry date","expiry dates","expiry"]},{"id":"backup","g":"run","word":"备份","text":"你的数据之前保存下来的一份副本，万一有东西丢了或坏了可以靠它。AI-gile 每天都会备份你的看板。","also":["backup","backups","back-up","back-ups"]},{"id":"restore","g":"run","word":"恢复","text":"从备份里把数据放回去。只有确认能恢复，备份才算真正有用，所以值得时不时试一下。","also":["还原","已恢复","restore","restored"]},{"id":"svcreview","g":"run","word":"服务回顾","text":"回看一项服务的一个月：开启和修复的事故、大家多快得到答复、正常运行时间，以及用掉的 token。月度回顾会为每项服务展示这些内容。","also":["服务回顾","月度回顾","每月回顾","service review","monthly review"]},{"id":"accessreview","g":"run","word":"访问审查","text":"每 90 天检查一次还有谁能进来：分享链接、好友的角色，以及保存的密钥。你逐一决定保留或移除，让旧的访问权限不会一直留着。","also":["访问审查","权限审查","access review","access reviews"]},{"id":"improvements","g":"run","word":"改进清单","text":"团队决定要做得更好的所有事情，集中在一个地方：回顾会的建议、复盘的行动项和问题的修复，每一项都带有状态。在 📈 改进 里就能找到。","also":["改进清单","改进","improvement list","improvements"]},{"id":"tokencap","g":"run","word":"Token 上限","text":"一项服务的智能体每月可用 token 的上限。达到 80% 时你会收到警告；达到 100% 时，它们的后台工作会等到下个月，或等你调高上限。","also":["token 上限","每月 token 上限","token cap","token caps"]},{"id":"svcmgmt","g":"run","word":"Service management","text":"Everything involved in keeping a service useful and working for the people who rely on it, day after day.","also":["managing a service"]},{"id":"itil","g":"run","word":"ITIL","text":"A widely used guide to good service management, covering things like incidents, problems and changes. The name once stood for IT Infrastructure Library."},{"id":"itsm","g":"run","word":"IT service management (ITSM)","text":"Running technology as a service for people, with clear ways to handle requests, incidents, changes and improvements.","also":["ITSM"]},{"id":"managed","g":"run","word":"Managed service","text":"A service someone else runs and looks after for you, under an agreement about how well it must work.","also":["managed services"]},{"id":"svcowner","g":"run","word":"Service owner","text":"The person who answers for a service as a whole: how well it works, what it costs and how it improves.","also":["service owners"]},{"id":"sla","g":"run","word":"Service level agreement (SLA)","text":"A written promise to customers about how well a service will work, like “up 99.9% of the time” or “answered within an hour”, often with money back if it is broken.","also":["SLA","SLAs","service level agreement"]},{"id":"slo","g":"run","word":"Service level objective (SLO)","text":"The team’s own target for how well a service should work over a period, usually set a bit stricter than the SLA to be safe.","also":["SLO","SLOs"]},{"id":"sli","g":"run","word":"Service level indicator (SLI)","text":"The actual measurement you check against a target, like the share of visits that loaded fine.","also":["SLI","SLIs"]},{"id":"availability","g":"run","word":"Availability","text":"Whether people can use a service when they need it, often measured as uptime.","also":["high availability"]},{"id":"downtime","g":"run","word":"Downtime","text":"Time when a service isn’t working or is switched off, planned or not.","also":["down time"]},{"id":"errorbudget","g":"run","word":"Error budget","text":"The small amount of failure your reliability target allows. While some is left you can take risks with changes; once it is used up, you slow down.","also":["error budgets"]},{"id":"outage","g":"run","word":"Outage","text":"A time when a service is down or badly broken for its users.","also":["outages"]},{"id":"degraded","g":"run","word":"Degraded","text":"Working, but badly: slow, or with some parts not working.","also":["degraded service"]},{"id":"monitoring","g":"run","word":"Monitoring","text":"Watching a service automatically all the time, so problems are spotted, ideally before users notice.","also":["monitored"]},{"id":"observability","g":"run","word":"Observability","text":"How well you can tell what is going on inside a system from the outside, using its logs, numbers and traces.","also":["o11y"]},{"id":"log","g":"run","word":"Log","text":"A running diary a program keeps of what it is doing, used to work out what went wrong.","also":["logs","logging"],"noTip":1},{"id":"trace","g":"run","word":"Trace","text":"A record of how one request travels through the different parts of a system, step by step, showing where it slowed down.","also":["traces","tracing"]},{"id":"latency","g":"run","word":"Latency","text":"How long something takes to answer, like the wait between clicking and the page appearing.","also":["lag"]},{"id":"alert","g":"run","word":"Alert","text":"An automatic message that something needs attention, like the website not answering.","also":["alerts","alerting"],"noTip":1},{"id":"alertfatigue","g":"run","word":"Alert fatigue","text":"When there are so many alerts that people start ignoring them, including the important ones.","also":["alarm fatigue"]},{"id":"dashboard","g":"run","word":"Dashboard","text":"A screen showing the most important numbers and statuses at a glance.","also":["dashboards"],"noTip":1},{"id":"paging","g":"run","word":"Paging","text":"Urgently calling or waking the on-call person about a problem.","also":["paged"]},{"id":"escalation","g":"run","word":"Escalation","text":"Bringing in someone with more skill or authority when an issue can’t be sorted at the current level, or not fast enough.","also":["escalate","escalated"]},{"id":"triage","g":"run","word":"Triage","text":"Quickly sorting incoming issues by how urgent and important they are, so the worst gets handled first.","also":["triaged"]},{"id":"impact","g":"run","word":"Impact","text":"How much something affects people: how many, and how badly.","noTip":1},{"id":"urgency","g":"run","word":"Urgency","text":"How quickly something needs to be dealt with. Impact and urgency together set the priority.","noTip":1},{"id":"plevels","g":"run","word":"Priority levels (P1, P2…)","text":"Labels for how urgent an issue is. P1 is the most serious and needs action straight away.","also":["P1","P2","P3","P4"]},{"id":"queue","g":"run","word":"Ticket queue","text":"The list of requests and incidents waiting to be picked up.","also":["ticket queues"]},{"id":"tiers","g":"run","word":"First line and second line","text":"Levels of support: first line takes the call and fixes simple things; second line has deeper skills for harder ones. Also called tier 1 and tier 2.","also":["first line","second line","first-line support","tier 1","tier 2"]},{"id":"ic","g":"run","word":"Incident commander","text":"The one person who leads the response to an incident, so everyone knows who decides and who does what.","also":["incident commanders"]},{"id":"responsetime","g":"run","word":"Response time","text":"How long it takes before someone first picks up a request or incident.","also":["response times"]},{"id":"resolution","g":"run","word":"Resolution time","text":"How long it takes to fully sort out a request or incident, from report to fixed.","also":["resolution times","time to resolve"]},{"id":"mttr","g":"run","word":"Mean time to recover (MTTR)","text":"On average, how long it takes to get a service working again after it breaks. MTTR can mean slightly different things, so say what you measure.","also":["MTTR","mean time to restore"]},{"id":"mtbf","g":"run","word":"Mean time between failures (MTBF)","text":"On average, how long a service runs before something breaks.","also":["MTBF"]},{"id":"cmdb","g":"run","word":"Configuration management database (CMDB)","text":"The list of all your configuration items and how they connect, so you can see what depends on what.","also":["CMDB"]},{"id":"asset","g":"run","word":"Asset","text":"Anything of value you own or pay for to run the service, like a laptop, a software licence or a domain.","also":["assets"],"noTip":1},{"id":"capplan","g":"run","word":"Capacity planning","text":"Making sure there will be enough computer power, storage and people for what is coming.","also":["capacity plan"]},{"id":"scaling","g":"run","word":"Scaling","text":"Coping with more users by giving a machine more power (scaling up) or adding more machines (scaling out).","also":["scale up","scale out","scaling up","scaling out"]},{"id":"bcp","g":"run","word":"Business continuity","text":"Plans for keeping the important things going when something big goes wrong, like losing an office, a key supplier or the computers.","also":["business continuity plan"]},{"id":"dr","g":"run","word":"Disaster recovery (DR)","text":"The plan and tools for getting a service back after something serious, like losing a whole data centre.","also":["disaster recovery"]},{"id":"rto","g":"run","word":"Recovery time objective (RTO)","text":"The longest a service may be down after a disaster before it must be working again.","also":["RTO"]},{"id":"rpo","g":"run","word":"Recovery point objective (RPO)","text":"How much recent data you could afford to lose, measured in time. An RPO of one hour means backups at least every hour.","also":["RPO"]},{"id":"redundancy","g":"run","word":"Redundancy","text":"Having spares ready, like a second server, so one failure doesn’t stop the service.","also":["redundant"]},{"id":"failover","g":"run","word":"Failover","text":"Switching over to a spare system by itself when the usual one fails.","also":["fail over"]},{"id":"spof","g":"run","word":"Single point of failure","text":"One part that takes everything down with it when it breaks, because there is no spare.","also":["single points of failure","SPOF"]},{"id":"maintwin","g":"run","word":"Maintenance window","text":"A planned time when a service may be paused or slow for upkeep, announced to users in advance.","also":["maintenance windows","planned maintenance"]},{"id":"handover","g":"run","word":"Handover","text":"Passing work, knowledge and responsibility to another person or team, so nothing gets dropped.","also":["handovers","hand over"]},{"id":"kb","g":"run","word":"Knowledge base","text":"A collection of help articles and answers, for users and for the team.","also":["knowledge bases"]},{"id":"csat","g":"run","word":"Customer satisfaction (CSAT)","text":"A score from users saying how happy they were, often asked after a request is closed.","also":["CSAT"]},{"id":"supplier","g":"run","word":"Supplier","text":"An outside company you rely on, like your hosting provider. Also called a vendor or third party.","also":["suppliers","vendor","vendors","third party"],"noTip":1},{"id":"retire","g":"run","word":"Service retirement","text":"Switching a service off for good, safely: telling its users, dealing with its data and access, and stopping its costs.","also":["retiring a service"]},{"id":"eol","g":"run","word":"End of life (EOL)","text":"When a product stops getting updates or support from its maker, so it should be replaced.","also":["EOL","end-of-life"]},{"id":"cybersec","g":"sec","word":"Cybersecurity","text":"Protecting systems and data from being broken into, stolen or damaged.","also":["cyber security"]},{"id":"authn","g":"sec","word":"Authentication","text":"Checking who someone is, usually with a password or a code. Often shortened to authn.","also":["authn"]},{"id":"authz","g":"sec","word":"Authorisation","text":"Checking what someone is allowed to do once you know who they are. Often shortened to authz.","also":["authz","authorization"]},{"id":"mfa","g":"sec","word":"Multi-factor authentication (MFA)","text":"Logging in with two or more proofs, like a password plus a code on your phone. Also called two-factor (2FA).","also":["MFA","2FA","two-factor","two-factor authentication"]},{"id":"sso","g":"sec","word":"Single sign-on (SSO)","text":"Logging in once to get into many systems, like using your work account everywhere.","also":["SSO"]},{"id":"pwmgr","g":"sec","word":"Password manager","text":"An app that creates strong passwords and remembers them for you.","also":["password managers"]},{"id":"secret","g":"sec","word":"Secret","text":"A password, key or other private code a program uses to get into something. It must never be shared or written into the code.","also":["secrets"],"noTip":1},{"id":"apikey","g":"sec","word":"API key","text":"A secret code that lets a program use another service’s API, and shows who is using (and paying for) it.","also":["API keys"]},{"id":"accesstoken","g":"sec","word":"Access token","text":"A temporary pass that proves a program is allowed in, so it doesn’t need your password each time. Not the same as an AI token.","also":["access tokens"]},{"id":"encryption","g":"sec","word":"Encryption","text":"Scrambling information so only someone with the right key can read it.","also":["encrypted","encrypt"]},{"id":"https","g":"sec","word":"HTTPS","text":"The secure way web pages travel, shown by the padlock in your browser, so nobody in between can read or change them."},{"id":"cert","g":"sec","word":"SSL/TLS certificate","text":"A digital ID card that lets a website prove who it is and use HTTPS. It expires, so it must be renewed.","also":["TLS certificate","SSL certificate","certificate","certificates"]},{"id":"permissions","g":"sec","word":"Permissions","text":"The rules for who may see or change what.","also":["permission"],"noTip":1},{"id":"leastpriv","g":"sec","word":"Least privilege","text":"Giving each person or program only the access they truly need, for only as long as they need it.","also":["principle of least privilege"]},{"id":"rbac","g":"sec","word":"Role-based access control (RBAC)","text":"Giving access by role, like viewer or editor, rather than person by person.","also":["RBAC","role-based access"]},{"id":"vuln","g":"sec","word":"Vulnerability","text":"A weakness that someone could use to break in or cause harm.","also":["vulnerabilities"]},{"id":"zeroday","g":"sec","word":"Zero-day","text":"A weakness attackers know about before the makers have had any time to fix it.","also":["zero-days","zero day"]},{"id":"malware","g":"sec","word":"Malware","text":"Harmful software, like viruses, made to damage, spy or steal.","also":["virus","viruses"]},{"id":"ransomware","g":"sec","word":"Ransomware","text":"Malware that locks up your files and demands money to unlock them. Good backups are the best defence.","also":["ransomware attack"]},{"id":"phishing","g":"sec","word":"Phishing","text":"Fake messages that try to trick you into giving away passwords or money, or opening something harmful.","also":["phishing email","phishing emails"]},{"id":"socialeng","g":"sec","word":"Social engineering","text":"Tricking people, rather than computers, into giving access, like a fake call from “IT support”."},{"id":"firewall","g":"sec","word":"Firewall","text":"A guard that decides which network traffic may come in or go out.","also":["firewalls"]},{"id":"vpn","g":"sec","word":"VPN","text":"Short for virtual private network: a private, scrambled link over the internet, often used to reach work systems safely.","also":["virtual private network"]},{"id":"ddos","g":"sec","word":"Denial of service (DoS, DDoS)","text":"Flooding a service with so much fake traffic that real people can’t use it. DDoS means it comes from many computers at once.","also":["DoS","DDoS","denial of service","DDoS attack"]},{"id":"pentest","g":"sec","word":"Penetration test (pen test)","text":"Hiring friendly experts to try to break in, so weaknesses are found before real attackers find them.","also":["pen test","pen testing","penetration testing","pentest"]},{"id":"audit","g":"sec","word":"Security audit","text":"A careful check of how well something is protected, against a list of good practice.","also":["security audits"]},{"id":"threatmodel","g":"sec","word":"Threat modelling","text":"Thinking through who might attack something, how, and what you would do to stop them.","also":["threat model","threat modeling"]},{"id":"breach","g":"sec","word":"Data breach","text":"When private information gets out to people who shouldn’t have it, or is lost or changed without permission.","also":["data breaches"]},{"id":"pii","g":"sec","word":"Personal data","text":"Any information about a person who can be identified, like a name, email address or location. Also called PII.","also":["PII","personally identifiable information"]},{"id":"gdpr","g":"sec","word":"GDPR","text":"The European law on personal data (the UK has its own version): collect only what you need, keep it safe, and let people see or delete theirs.","also":["General Data Protection Regulation"]},{"id":"dataprot","g":"sec","word":"Data protection","text":"Looking after personal data properly and within the law.","also":["data protection law"]},{"id":"privacypolicy","g":"sec","word":"Privacy policy","text":"A public page saying what personal data you collect, why, and what you do with it.","also":["privacy statement","privacy notice"]},{"id":"consent","g":"sec","word":"Consent","text":"A person’s clear, free yes to their data being used in a particular way. They can take it back at any time.","also":["opt-in"],"noTip":1},{"id":"cookie","g":"sec","word":"Cookie","text":"A small file a website saves in your browser to remember things, like being logged in. Some need your consent first.","also":["cookies"],"noTip":1},{"id":"minimisation","g":"sec","word":"Data minimisation","text":"Collecting and keeping only the personal data you really need for the job.","also":["data minimization"]},{"id":"retention","g":"sec","word":"Data retention","text":"How long data is kept before it is deleted. Keeping it no longer than needed is safer and often the law.","also":["retention period"]},{"id":"anon","g":"sec","word":"Anonymisation","text":"Changing data so nobody can reasonably be identified from it any more.","also":["anonymised","anonymization","anonymous data"]},{"id":"pseudo","g":"sec","word":"Pseudonymisation","text":"Swapping names and other details for codes, with a key kept apart to link them back. It is still personal data.","also":["pseudonymised","pseudonymization"]},{"id":"dpa","g":"sec","word":"Data processing agreement (DPA)","text":"A contract saying how a supplier may handle personal data on your behalf.","also":["DPA"]},{"id":"dpia","g":"sec","word":"Data protection impact assessment (DPIA)","text":"A careful look, before you start, at how a plan to use personal data could affect people and how to lower the risks.","also":["DPIA"]},{"id":"auditlog","g":"sec","word":"Audit log","text":"A record of who did what and when, so actions can be checked later.","also":["audit logs","audit trail"]},{"id":"compliance","g":"sec","word":"Compliance","text":"Following the laws, rules and standards that apply to you, and being able to show that you do.","also":["compliant"]},{"id":"iso","g":"sec","word":"ISO 27001","text":"A well known international standard for managing information security. Companies can be certified against it.","also":["ISO/IEC 27001"]},{"id":"soc","g":"sec","word":"SOC 2","text":"A US report, checked by outside auditors, showing a company looks after customer data properly.","also":["SOC2"]},{"id":"agent","g":"ai","word":"智能体","text":"独立处理任务的 AI：读代码、做改动、运行检查，并汇报结果。","also":["agents","Agent","agent"]},{"id":"token","g":"ai","word":"Token","text":"AI 用量的计量单位：大约相当于读写的几个字符。Token 越少，套餐剩得越多。","also":["tokens"]},{"id":"hook","g":"ai","word":"钩子","text":"一条会在设定时刻自动运行的小命令，例如 AI 会话开始时，这样团队才能听到你的评论。","also":["hooks","Hook","hook"]},{"id":"ai","g":"ai","word":"Artificial intelligence (AI)","text":"Computer systems that do things we usually think need a person, like writing, answering questions or recognising pictures.","also":["AI","artificial intelligence"],"noTip":1},{"id":"ml","g":"ai","word":"Machine learning (ML)","text":"A way of making computers learn patterns from lots of examples, instead of giving them exact rules.","also":["machine learning"]},{"id":"model","g":"ai","word":"Model","text":"The trained “brain” of an AI system, built by learning from huge numbers of examples. Models differ in skill, speed and price.","also":["models","AI model"],"noTip":1},{"id":"llm","g":"ai","word":"Large language model (LLM)","text":"An AI model trained on vast amounts of text, so it can read and write language.","also":["LLM","LLMs"]},{"id":"genai","g":"ai","word":"Generative AI","text":"AI that creates new things, like text, pictures or code, rather than only sorting or spotting them.","also":["GenAI"]},{"id":"chatbot","g":"ai","word":"Chatbot","text":"A program you talk with in everyday language, by typing or speaking.","also":["chatbots"]},{"id":"prompt","g":"ai","word":"Prompt","text":"What you give an AI: your question or instructions, plus anything you hand it. Clearer prompts usually get better answers.","also":["prompts"]},{"id":"prompteng","g":"ai","word":"Prompt engineering","text":"The skill of writing prompts that get reliable, useful results.","also":["prompting"]},{"id":"sysprompt","g":"ai","word":"System prompt","text":"Background instructions an AI gets before the conversation starts, setting its role and rules. They weigh more than what you type.","also":["system prompts","system instruction","system instructions"]},{"id":"context","g":"ai","word":"Context","text":"Everything the AI can see while it works: the instructions, your messages, files it has read and earlier replies.","noTip":1},{"id":"ctxwin","g":"ai","word":"Context window","text":"How much an AI can keep in mind at once, measured in tokens. Past that, older parts get dropped or summarised.","also":["context windows"]},{"id":"session","g":"ai","word":"Session","text":"One working conversation with an AI, from start to finish. A new session starts with a fresh memory.","also":["sessions"],"noTip":1},{"id":"memory","g":"ai","word":"Memory","text":"Notes an AI keeps between sessions, so it remembers your preferences and past decisions.","noTip":1},{"id":"hallucination","g":"ai","word":"Hallucination","text":"When an AI says something that sounds sure of itself but is made up or wrong. Check important facts.","also":["hallucinations","hallucinate","hallucinates"]},{"id":"training","g":"ai","word":"Training","text":"Teaching a model by showing it huge numbers of examples, before anyone uses it.","also":["trained"],"noTip":1},{"id":"trainingdata","g":"ai","word":"Training data","text":"The examples a model learned from. Its knowledge stops at the date that data was collected.","also":["training set"]},{"id":"cutoff","g":"ai","word":"Knowledge cutoff","text":"The date an AI’s built-in knowledge ends. It won’t know about later things unless it looks them up.","also":["training cutoff"]},{"id":"finetune","g":"ai","word":"Fine-tuning","text":"Extra training of an existing model on your own examples, to make it better at one particular job.","also":["fine-tune","fine-tuned","fine tuning"]},{"id":"inference","g":"ai","word":"Inference","text":"An AI model actually being used to answer something, as opposed to being trained.","also":["model inference"]},{"id":"tooluse","g":"ai","word":"Tool use","text":"An AI being able to do things, not just talk: run commands, read files or search the web, through tools the app gives it.","also":["tool calling","tool call","tool calls"]},{"id":"subagent","g":"ai","word":"Subagent","text":"A helper AI that a main AI hands a smaller job to, and gets the result back from.","also":["subagents","sub-agent"]},{"id":"multiagent","g":"ai","word":"Multi-agent","text":"Several AIs working together on one piece of work, each with its own part, like a small team.","also":["multi-agent system"]},{"id":"autonomy","g":"ai","word":"Autonomy","text":"How much an AI may do on its own before it has to ask a person.","also":["autonomous"]},{"id":"hitl","g":"ai","word":"Human in the loop","text":"Keeping a person involved to check or approve what the AI does at key points. In AI-gile that is you.","also":["human-in-the-loop","HITL"]},{"id":"guardrails","g":"ai","word":"Guardrails","text":"Limits and checks that keep an AI’s actions safe, like needing approval before anything goes live. They lower the risk of mistakes, but don’t rule them out.","also":["guardrail"]},{"id":"rag","g":"ai","word":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)","text":"Letting an AI look things up in your own documents before it answers, so its answers rest on your facts.","also":["RAG","retrieval augmented generation"]},{"id":"embedding","g":"ai","word":"Embedding","text":"Turning text into a list of numbers that captures its meaning, so a computer can find things that mean similar things.","also":["embeddings"]},{"id":"vectordb","g":"ai","word":"Vector database","text":"A database built to search those lists of numbers (embeddings), to find material with a similar meaning.","also":["vector databases","vector store"]},{"id":"mcp","g":"ai","word":"Model Context Protocol (MCP)","text":"A common way to plug tools and data sources into AI apps, so an AI can use them.","also":["MCP"]},{"id":"temperature","g":"ai","word":"Temperature","text":"A setting for how adventurous an AI’s answers are. Low gives steadier, more predictable answers; high gives more varied ones. Neither makes it always right.","also":["model temperature"],"noTip":1},{"id":"multimodal","g":"ai","word":"Multimodal","text":"Able to work with more than one kind of information, such as text, pictures and sound.","also":["multi-modal"]},{"id":"evals","g":"ai","word":"Evaluation (eval)","text":"A planned test of how well an AI does on chosen examples, so you can compare models or prompts.","also":["eval","evals","evaluations"]},{"id":"bias","g":"ai","word":"Bias","text":"When an AI (or a person) leans unfairly one way, often because of what it learned from.","also":["biased"],"noTip":1},{"id":"responsible","g":"ai","word":"Responsible AI","text":"Using AI fairly, safely and honestly, with people still answerable for the results.","also":["ethical AI"]},{"id":"injection","g":"ai","word":"Prompt injection","text":"A trick where hidden instructions in a web page or file try to make an AI do something its user didn’t ask for.","also":["prompt injections"]},{"id":"ratelimit","g":"ai","word":"Rate limit","text":"A cap on how many requests you may send to a service in a set time. Go over it and you have to wait.","also":["rate limits","rate limited"]},{"id":"usagelimit","g":"ai","word":"Usage limit","text":"How much AI work your plan allows in a period, after which it pauses or costs more.","also":["usage limits"]},{"id":"vibe","g":"ai","word":"Vibe coding","text":"Building software mostly by describing what you want to an AI and letting it write the code.","also":["vibe-coding"]},{"id":"budget","g":"money","word":"Budget","text":"The amount of money set aside for something.","also":["budgets"],"noTip":1},{"id":"costest","g":"money","word":"Cost estimate","text":"A best guess at what something will cost before you start.","also":["cost estimates"]},{"id":"roi","g":"money","word":"Return on investment (ROI)","text":"What you get back compared with what you put in. If it is positive, it was worth it.","also":["ROI"]},{"id":"tco","g":"money","word":"Total cost of ownership (TCO)","text":"Everything something really costs over its whole life, not just the price to buy it: running, support, upgrades and finally retiring it.","also":["TCO"]},{"id":"capex","g":"money","word":"CapEx and OpEx","text":"CapEx is money spent up front on things you keep; OpEx is ongoing spending, like monthly bills.","also":["CapEx","OpEx","capital expenditure","operating expenditure"]},{"id":"subscription","g":"money","word":"Subscription","text":"Paying regularly, usually monthly or yearly, to keep using something.","also":["subscriptions"],"noTip":1},{"id":"saas","g":"money","word":"Software as a service (SaaS)","text":"Software you use online and pay for by subscription, while the maker runs it for you.","also":["SaaS"]},{"id":"payg","g":"money","word":"Pay as you go","text":"Paying only for what you use, like cloud computing or AI tokens.","also":["pay-as-you-go","usage-based pricing"]},{"id":"overage","g":"money","word":"Overage","text":"Use above what your plan includes, which can cost extra.","also":["overages","overage charges"]},{"id":"billingalert","g":"money","word":"Billing alert","text":"A message when spending or use reaches a level you chose. It warns you, but doesn’t always stop further charges.","also":["billing alerts","spending alert"]},{"id":"freetier","g":"money","word":"Free tier","text":"A free level of a paid service, with limits. Handy for starting out.","also":["free plan"]},{"id":"pricing","g":"money","word":"Pricing plan","text":"The different packages a service sells, each with its own price and limits.","also":["pricing plans","price plan"]},{"id":"licence","g":"money","word":"Licence","text":"The rules for how you may use, copy, change or share something someone else made, such as software, fonts or pictures.","also":["licences","license","licenses"],"noTip":1},{"id":"opensource","g":"money","word":"Open source","text":"Software whose code anyone can read, use and often change for free, under the rules of its licence.","also":["open-source","open source software","OSS"]},{"id":"osslicence","g":"money","word":"Open source licence","text":"The rules that come with open source code. Some let you do almost anything (like MIT); some ask you to share your changes too (like GPL).","also":["open source licences","MIT licence","GPL"]},{"id":"proprietary","g":"money","word":"Proprietary","text":"Owned by a company and not free to copy or change. The opposite of open source.","also":["proprietary software"]},{"id":"copyright","g":"money","word":"Copyright","text":"The legal right of a creator to control copies of their work, including code and writing.","also":["copyrighted"],"noTip":1},{"id":"ip","g":"money","word":"Intellectual property (IP)","text":"Ideas and creations the law treats as owned, like code, designs, names and logos.","also":["intellectual property"]},{"id":"tos","g":"money","word":"Terms of service","text":"The rules you agree to when you use a service.","also":["terms and conditions","terms of use"]},{"id":"contract","g":"money","word":"Contract","text":"A written agreement between two sides that the law will back up.","also":["contracts"],"noTip":1},{"id":"sow","g":"money","word":"Statement of work (SOW)","text":"A document setting out exactly what work will be done, by when and for how much.","also":["SOW"]},{"id":"fixedprice","g":"money","word":"Fixed price","text":"An agreed price for the whole job, however long it takes.","also":["fixed-price"]},{"id":"tm","g":"money","word":"Time and materials","text":"Paying for the hours really worked and the things really used, rather than a set price.","also":["T&M"]},{"id":"invoice","g":"money","word":"Invoice","text":"A bill asking for payment for work done or goods supplied.","also":["invoices"],"noTip":1},{"id":"lockin","g":"money","word":"Vendor lock-in","text":"When moving away from a supplier is so hard or costly that you are stuck with them.","also":["lock-in"]},{"id":"procurement","g":"money","word":"Procurement","text":"The process of choosing and buying from suppliers.","also":["purchasing"]},{"id":"servicecredit","g":"money","word":"Service credit","text":"Money back or a discount you get when a supplier misses its SLA.","also":["service credits"]}]}};
